index e8afe5a..1afe536 100644
--- a/docs/configuration.md
+++ b/docs/configuration.md
@@ -837,15 +837,46 @@ Manage records without starting a model, from the project directory:
 
 ```sh
 kiln memory export                 # JSON, including correction history
+kiln memory import backup.json     # import an export into this project (see below)
 kiln memory forget test-runner     # deletes every version of this key
 kiln memory restore 12             # restores this project's version as a new record
 ```
 
+`kiln memory [global] import <file> [--dry-run] [--on-conflict error|skip|replace]`
+transfers an export produced by `kiln memory export` into the current project
+scope (or, with `global`, the user preferences scope). It is a local command and
+never calls a model. The input is exactly one JSON array in the export format:
+malformed JSON, trailing content, a non-array or `null`, unknown record or source
+fields, files over 4 MiB, and more than 1000 records are all rejected; an empty
+array is valid. An export carries correction history, so for each key the
+record with the largest input id becomes the current version and the rest are
+validated but not replayed. Imported scope, created, and supersedes values are
+never trusted as destination identity or lineage — records are written into
+*this* project's scope with fresh ids and creation times, preserving only their
+expiry. Input ids must be positive and unique.
+
+Source-bound project records are rebound into the current worktree: each
+source's root-relative path is resolved under this project's root and its
+SHA-256 is checked against the destination file. A missing, changed, oversized,
+non-regular, or escaping source (including on a historical version) rejects the
+whole import. Global import accepts preference records without sources only.
+
+A conflict is any key that already has an active record here (even expired or
+with a stale source). `--on-conflict error` (the default) rejects the whole
+batch; `skip` leaves existing keys untouched; `replace` supersedes them while
+preserving local correction history. The whole selected batch applies in one
+transaction, so any validation, conflict, or write failure leaves every memory,
+search entry, and source binding unchanged. `--dry-run` runs the same validation
+and conflict analysis but writes nothing. On success the command prints one JSON
+object — `{"inserted":N,"replaced":N,"skipped":N,"dry_run":false}` — counting
+selected current keys, not historical rows.
+
 Explicit user preferences can be shared across projects:
 
 ```sh
 kiln memory global remember response-style "Prefer concise answers"
 kiln memory global export
+kiln memory global import prefs.json
 kiln memory global forget response-style
 kiln memory global restore 12
 ```
